Assume for this practice exercise that you are investigating a race condition in a hypothetical offline-first sync service written in Rust. Consider this minimal self-contained example:

fn apply_event(events: &mut Vec<Event>, id: u64, payload: Option<Payload>) -> Result<(), SyncError> {
    if let Some(existing) = events.iter().find(|e| e.id == id) {
        existing.payload = payload;
        return Ok(());
    }
    events.push(Event { id, payload });
    Ok(())
}

// Hypothetical failure trace: Two threads call apply_event for the same ID simultaneously.
// Observed behavior: Duplicate delivery charges occur in production logs.
// Expected behavior: Only one charge per unique delivery event ID.

Investigate why the provided code might fail to prevent duplicate charges under concurrent access. Identify what additional evidence is needed to confirm a race condition versus a logic error, and propose a verification step.

